phpBugTracker "2025" INSTALL
============================

TL;DR Version:
---------------------
 o unzip the code
 o edit and run setperms.sh
 o create your database backend
 o edit and run PEAR_TEST.php
 o point your browser to where you installed phpBugTracker
 o answer the configuration form fields
 o copy the downloaded config.php to where you installed phpBugTracker
 o reload the browser and you are logged in as the administrator


1. Preparation
------------------------------------------------------
 o  Download and unzip the latest phpbugtracker "2025" release zip file

 o  Set the perms and ownership on all the directories.

    $ vi setperms.sh            # set install dir, webserver user+group
    root# bash ./setperms.sh

    If you are on a system with SELinux, setperms.sh will set the proper context 
    on writable directories

 o  If using SQLite3 for the database, you do not have to create the database
    beforehand. You can specify the location and database name in the browser 
    installation form field and phpBugTracker will create it for you. It is 
    recommended to install it in the ./db directory such as "phpbugtracker.db".
    Directory "db" needs to be writable for SQLite3 usage. Also the proper 
    SELinux context will need to be be set on directory "db" (via setperms.sh):

    drwx------. 2 apache nobody unconfined_u:object_r:httpd_sys_rw_content_t:s0 4096 May 20 21:03 db
                                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    This is all handled by the setperms.sh script.

 o  If not using SQLite3 for the database, create your database. 
    See README-DATABASES for examples.

 o  Test if PHP via Composer pear/db PEAR::DB packages can connect and run a 
    simple query sucessfully to your newly created database with the user 
    credentitals, see PEAR_TEST.php

    You must first configure PEAR_TEST.php to use your database type and 
    authentication criteria . Afterwards you can run the test from either the
    command line or the browser:

    $ php PEAR_TEST.php
         -or-
    URL http://localhost/path/to/phpbugtracker/PEAR_TEST.php

 o  When PEAR_TEST.php can sucessfully connect and query your database, proceed
    to the next step.

2. phpBugTracker Setup Configuration
------------------------------------------------------
 o  Now point your browser to where you installed phpBugTracker: 
      URL http://localhost/path/to/phpbugtracker/install.php

 o  Fill in the install form fields. 

    Test your database connection again with the [Test Database] button using 
    database the connection parameters filled in one more time.

    If the database test connection was successful, fill in the rest of the form
    and click Submit

    Clicking Submit, phpBugTracker will install the database schema and load the
    tables with its information.  If the installation fails it will display the
    results of the schema and table loads for further information of what went
    wrong.

    If the installation is sucessful you will stay on the same page looking like
    nothing happened, but a generated configuration file named "config.php" will
    be downloaded via the browser. Check your Downloads directory. Copy this 
    generated "config.php" file to the root of the phpBugTracker directory.

    Reload your browser and it will redirect you to phpBugTracker's main page
    where you will logged in as the administrator.

    If for any reason you want to start all over and try another install after
    you have installed the generated downloaded config.php, then delete your
    config.php, delete your database, create a fresh new database, and go 
    through the install.php again.

3. Post Install
------------------------------------------------------
 o  Create your first project and components: Admin->Projects

 o  *NOTE* Before creating any user accounts, decide whether you want the user
           login to be a username or the users email address. Changing it 
           afterwards results in mixed name usage of login names and email
           addresses in reports. Default is users email address.

    This is toggled in Admin->Configuration:EMAIL_IS_LOGIN [X] Yes [ ] No


==> You are now ready to start using phpBugTracker! <==


4. Optional Post Configuration via config.php:
------------------------------------------------------
 o  All bug dates are stored and displayed by default in UTC time. 
    If you want bugs displayed in your local timezone set TZ_LOCAL in config.php
    accordingly.

 o  If you want on the main page phpBugTracker to display BOTH the table stats
    and the JpGraph Pie Chart stats, enable/disable in 
       config.php:USE_BOTH_JPGRAPH_AND_TABLE_STATS
    Display of JpGraph or Stats toggle are set in:
       Admin->Configuration:USE_JPGRAPH settings.

 o  E-mail Notifications
    phpBugTracker by default will use PHPMailer for both SMTP and local mail
    when installed. If you need SMTP deliveries, then enable it in 
    config.php:SMTP_EMAIL and its related settings found there.

    If you don't wish to use PHPMailer and instead use phpBugTracker's own mail
    routines (highly not recommended, it "works" but will be removed at a later
    date). This can be configured in config.php. This code was left in for
    historical reasons and will probably be removed at a later date.

 o  (optional) DB_CHARSET if needed (config.php). This is the client connection
    charset to the database.  This is database specific and not needed for most
    databases and PEAR::DB may not have this provision. Valid for Interbase, 
    Oracle, Sybase databases.

5. Other
------------------------------------------------------
 o  If you want to log all SQL queries for any debugging purposes generated by
    the PEAR::DB package, then edit vendor/pear/db/DB/common.php and enable it,
    line 1225:
      if (false) error_log(__FILE__."::".__FUNCTION__."():".__LINE__." calling this->simpleQuery(".$query.")");
    Logging is logged via PHP error_log() function.
    This is custom code locally and not included or part of PEAR::DB.
    Upgrading the PEAR::DB package will loose this code addition.

    SQL statements logged will generally show a lowercase syntax part and an
    uppercase syntax part. Lowercase come from from dynamically generated SQL
    code from .php files. The uppercase part of the SQL query comes from 
    ./in/db/<dbtype>.in template so you can see who does what part of the SQL
    query.

 o  NOTICE: Updating Composer packages
    If you are using Oracle database: 
       DO NOT update composer package PEAR::DB. Updating will break the queries.

       There is a local bug fix in the oci8.php code for the queries to work:
           vendor/pear/db/DB/oci8.php     see the diffs if curious.

       This bug has been reported to the maintainers as a critical fix and
       whether they do anything
       with it remains to be seen.

    If you are using SQLite3 for the database: 
       Do not update composer package PEAR::DB. This currently contains a local
       bug fix that is not crtical and has been reported to the maintainers. The
       local fix pertains to the case when it cannot open the database and its
       error handling. Upgrading the PEAR::DB package will loose the change.

       There are local bug fix code in:
           vendor/pear/db/DB/sqlite3.php  see the diffs if curious


